        <div id="motor-capacity-claims" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>How to Read THERUN’s Motor, Capacity and Noise Claims</h2>
<p>
            THERUN’s listings lead with three kinds of number: horsepower, weight capacity and noise. All three are the
            seller’s claims, none has been independently measured that we could find, and each needs a little
            translation before it is useful.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Horsepower: one figure, no context</h3>
          <p>
            Every THERUN prints a single figure: 2.5 HP on the walking pads, 3.0 HP on the TR06 and NEW-TR07, 3.5 or 4.0
            HP on the TR04 and 3.5 HP on the flagship. None says whether that is a continuous or a peak rating. On budget
            treadmills an unlabelled figure is almost always a peak, the brief maximum the motor reaches under load,
            rather than what it sustains during a 45-minute walk. Brands that publish a continuous rating, such as Merach,
            often show a continuous figure well under half the peak one. Treat THERUN’s figures as peak ratings and do not
            compare them with a continuous rating on another brand. Our <a href="/treadmill-horsepower-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill horsepower guide</a> explains why the continuous figure is the one that predicts heat and wear.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Capacity: buy on the lowest number</h3>
          <p>
            A capacity figure sums up the frame, deck, rollers and motor together. When one listing gives two figures, or
            two listings for the same model code give different ones, the lower figure is the safer one to plan on, and
            the usual advice is to leave a margin of around a quarter between your weight and the rating. On THERUN’s
            range that means planning on 300 lb for every folding treadmill, 265 lb for the YT05 and the 40-inch pad, and
            treating the 330 lb incline pad and the 400 lb TR04 as claims that need confirming with the seller. Walking
            pads in particular carry high capacity claims on light frames, and long daily sessions near the limit are
            what shorten their lives.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Noise: the motor is rarely the loudest part</h3>
          <p>
            The walking pads claim about 45 dB and the Manual Incline Treadmill 55 dB. Neither listing says how, where or
            at what speed those figures were measured. On any treadmill, footfall travelling into the floor is usually
            louder than motor hum, especially upstairs or on a timber floor. Plan for ordinary treadmill noise, use a
            dense mat, keep the belt lubricated, and test the machine at your real walking speed within the return window.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Apps and screens</h3>
          <p>
            The flagship and the TR04 listings name app support; the flagship names Zwift, FitShow and <a href="/best-treadmills-with-kinomap/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>Kinomap</a>. Zwift and
            Kinomap are separate services with their own memberships, and none is needed to use the treadmill. The
            cheaper TR06 and NEW-TR07 listings describe large LED displays and preset programmes but do not name an app.
            Buy any THERUN for what it does from its own console, and treat app support as a bonus.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="warranty" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>THERUN Warranty, Support and Returns</h2>
<p>
            <strong>Every THERUN listing checked gives a one-year warranty</strong> in its Amazon product information
            (checked November 2026). Nine say “1 Year”, “1 year” or “1 year warranty”, and the TR04 400 lb listing says “1
            Year Manufacturer”. None of the listings we read separates frame, motor and parts, and we could not reach a
            THERUN website with a written warranty policy, so we cannot say whether the frame or motor gets longer cover.
            Plan on one year for everything.
          </p>
          <p>
            One year is short but normal for this price. Most walking pads under $150 carry one year, and many budget
            folding treadmills do too. It becomes a weakness at the top of THERUN’s range: at about $800, the flagship
            competes with machines from established brands that commonly offer longer motor and frame cover, and that
            difference is worth more than a 20% incline to most buyers. Our <a href="/treadmill-warranty-guide/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">treadmill warranty guide</a> sets out what longer cover looks like and the questions to ask before you buy.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">How to make a claim</h3>
          <p>
            Because THERUN appears to have little support outside Amazon, the practical route for most owners is through
            the Amazon order: use “Get product support” or contact the seller from your order page, quote the ASIN and the
            model code, and describe the fault with photos or a short video. Keep the order confirmation, since a
            warranty runs from the date of purchase and proof of purchase is what a claim rests on. Check your manual
            for a contact address too; it is the most reliable place to find the after-sales email for your model.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Returns</h3>
          <p>
            On listings sold by Amazon.com, Amazon’s return policy applies, and the return window is shown on the listing
            and in your order. On listings sold by THERUN US Direct, the seller’s own terms apply; read them before you
            order. Either way, use the window properly. Walk on the machine at your usual speed for your usual session
            length in the first days, raise and lower the incline through its range, and fold and unfold it. Problems
            with belt tracking, incline motors and controllers tend to show up under real use, and finding one inside the
            return window is far easier than relying on a warranty claim afterwards. Keep the box until the window
            closes.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="who-it-suits" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>Who Should Buy a THERUN, and Who Should Skip It?</h2>
<ul>
            <li><strong>Buy one for incline walking on a budget.</strong> The TR06 12% treadmill gives powered incline,
            9 mph and a folding frame for under $300, sold by Amazon.com. That is a combination few established brands
            offer at the price.</li>
            <li><strong>Buy one for slow desk walking.</strong> The <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-handrail/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>2-in-1 walking pad</a> with the adjustable handlebar is a
            cheap, low-risk way to find out whether you will actually walk at your desk.</li>
            <li><strong>Buy one if you need it to fold away.</strong> Every THERUN folds, and the walking pads are under
            5 inches tall in their product information, low enough to slide under most sofas.</li>
            <li><strong>Skip the cheap models if you run.</strong> Below the flagship, no THERUN has a belt longer than
            47.2 inches. Running needs about 55 to 60 inches; our guide to <a href="/best-folding-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">the best folding treadmills</a> lists folding machines with running-length decks.</li>
            <li><strong>Think twice if you are near 300 lb.</strong> The 400 lb and 330 lb claims conflict with other
            THERUN listings or are unconfirmed. A machine with a consistent, higher rating and a heavier frame is the
            safer buy.</li>
            <li><strong>Look elsewhere if you want long support.</strong> One year of cover, no published continuous motor
            ratings and little support outside Amazon add up to a machine you should treat as a few years of use, not a
            decade.</li>
          </ul>
          <p>
            The honest summary: THERUN is good value at the bottom of its range and ordinary value at the top. The
            cheaper the THERUN, the easier it is to recommend, because the risks are proportionate to the price.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div id="alternatives" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>THERUN vs Merach, UREVO and Other Amazon Brands</h2>
<p>
            THERUN is cross-shopped mostly against the other brands that dominate Amazon’s treadmill and walking pad
            searches. Our guide to <a href="/merach-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">Merach treadmills</a> covers a brand whose pads cost more, around $300, but whose pages publish continuous motor ratings (1.25 HP on
            its W50 TrekPad) and whose warranty can reach two years if you register. Our <a href="/urevo-treadmills/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium">UREVO treadmills</a> guide covers another
            Amazon-first brand with a wider range of walking pads and 2-in-1s and a warranty with conditions of its own.
          </p>
          <p>
            Against those, THERUN’s case is simple: price. Its TR06 gets you powered incline for less than most rivals
            charge for a flat pad with a handlebar, and its walking pads start under $90. What you give up is
            transparency. Merach and UREVO publish more detail and run their own stores with their own return terms;
            THERUN publishes listing copy and leaves support largely to Amazon.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">When to spend more instead</h3>
          <p>
            If you want to run, the flagship’s 55 by 20-inch belt and 12 mph are the minimum, and at about $800 you are in
            the price range of folding running treadmills from long-established brands with longer warranties and dealer
            or in-home service. If you are a heavier user, look for a machine whose capacity is stated consistently and
            whose frame weight backs it up. And if you want incline walking only, compare the TR06 with the <a href="/best-walking-pads-with-incline/" class="text-[#0F62FE] font-medium" data-autolink>incline
            walking pads</a> from Merach and UREVO, which cost more but publish more of the numbers that matter.
          </p>
          <h3 class="text-xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Cheaper than a THERUN</h3>
          <p>
            There is not much below THERUN’s $80 YT05 that is worth buying, and below about $80 most walking pads drop
            the remote, the shock absorption or any clear capacity figure. If THERUN’s walking pads are already your
            budget, the best saving is not a cheaper brand but the right THERUN listing: one sold by Amazon.com, with
            consistent figures, at a price near the bottom of its recent range.
          </p>
        </div>

        <!-- Common mistakes -->
        <div id="common-mistakes" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2>4 Mistakes People Make Buying a THERUN Treadmill</h2>
          <p>THERUN can be good value. These are the mistakes that turn a cheap treadmill into a poor one.</p>
          <ol class="ed-mistakes not-prose">
            <li>
              <h3>Trusting the title over the bullets</h3>
              <p>THERUN titles sometimes carry a higher capacity or larger belt than the bullets below them. Read both, and where they differ, plan on the lower figure: 300 lb for the TR06, 265 lb and a 35.5-inch belt for the YT05.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Buying a short belt to run on</h3>
              <p>Every THERUN except the $800 flagship has a 47.2-inch belt or shorter. That is fine for walking and light jogging, but a running stride needs about 55 to 60 inches. If you plan to run, buy the flagship or another brand’s running deck.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Ignoring who the seller is</h3>
              <p>Most THERUN listings are sold by Amazon.com, but two are sold by THERUN US Direct, and one showed a second offer about $106 dearer. Check “Sold by” and the price of the offer in your basket, and read the seller’s return terms on a third-party listing.</p>
            </li>
            <li>
              <h3>Letting the return window run out unused</h3>
              <p>With one year of cover and little support outside Amazon, the return window is your best protection. Use the machine at your real speed, session length and incline in the first days, and keep the box until the window closes.</p>
            </li>
          </ol>
        </div>

        <!-- The bottom line -->
        <div id="bottom-line" class="ed-bottomline">
          <h2>Our verdict on the brand</h2>
          <p>
            <strong class="text-white">THERUN is a legitimate budget brand whose listings you should read line by line.</strong> Its machines are real, most are sold by Amazon.com, and each carries a one-year warranty. The TR06 12% Auto
          Incline Treadmill at about $285 is the one most buyers should consider, and the $88 2-in-1 walking pad is a
          cheap way to start desk walking.
          </p>
          <p>
            Plan on the lowest capacity any listing gives, treat every horsepower figure as a peak, and use the return
          window. If you want to run, or want longer cover than a year, compare <a href="/merach-treadmills/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">Merach</a> or the <a href="/best-treadmills-on-amazon/" class="text-[#5AA9FF] font-bold no-underline">best treadmills on Amazon</a> before you buy.
          </p>
        </div>

